      "content": "Apologies for the mystical misdirection. Let's get down to the brass tacks of technical troubleshooting.\n\nTo inspect the host's `dmesg` or `journalctl` from within your Jenkins agent, you can indeed access these logs directly from the host system. Since Jenkins agents run in Docker containers, you can execute commands within the container to access the host's logs. Here's how you can do it:\n\n1. **Accessing `dmesg` from the Jenkins Agent Container**:\n   You can use the `docker exec` command to run `dmesg` inside the container. This will display the kernel ring buffer messages, which include OOM killer activity.\n\n   ```bash\n   docker exec -it jenkins-agent dmesg | grep -i \"oom\"\n   ```\n\n2. **Accessing `journalctl` from the Jenkins Agent Container**:\n   Similarly, you can use `journalctl` to view the system journal, which may contain OOM killer messages.\n\n   ```bash\n   docker exec -it jenkins-agent journalctl -k | grep -i \"oom\"\n   ```\n\n3. **Accessing Host Logs Directly**:\n   If you prefer to check the host's logs directly,",
